<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Widukind of Corvey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 925–after 973</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/widukind">Widukind</a> of Corvey was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> monk of the abbey of Corvey and author of the Deeds of the Saxons (Res gestae Saxonicae), written in the late 960s and early 970s. It is a chief source for the Ottonian kings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a> and Otto I, including Otto's victory at the Lechfeld in 955, and for the eastern frontier under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gero-the-great">Margrave Gero</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/widukind-of-corvey.jpg" alt="Widukind of Corvey" width="151" height="189" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WIDUKIND.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Almost everything known about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/widukind">Widukind</a> comes from his own work. The only contemporary outside reference is a list of monks who entered Corvey during the abbacy of Folcmar (917 to 942), in which he appears second to last among fifty entrants. His birth is traditionally placed around 920 to 925, though some scholars, reading the register as recording a boy of six to eight, date it to about 933 to 935. His family is unknown, but the prominence of Corvey and its close ties to the king suggest that admission needed a background of some importance, and his interest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> history makes a Saxon family highly probable.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/widukind">Widukind</a> entered the community at Corvey about 940 and began as a writer of saints' lives. He is credited with lives of Saint Paul and Saint Thecla, based on the Acts of Paul and Thecla, but nothing of them survives. At an uncertain and disputed date he composed his principal work. He probably began it at the request of Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matilda-of-ringelheim">Matilda of Ringelheim</a>, mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i">Otto I</a>, though Archbishop William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, Otto's son, has also been proposed as patron. Both died in March 968, after which Widukind dedicated the work to Abbess Mathilde of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quedlinburg">Quedlinburg</a>, Otto's daughter, born in 955. No record of his death survives; he was alive after finishing a history that ends with Otto's death in May 973.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The Res gestae Saxonicae, the Deeds of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a>, in three books, is modelled on the Roman historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sallust">Sallust</a> and the Books of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maccabees">Maccabees</a>. Widukind wrote as a Saxon, proud of his people, and began not with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> but with a brief synopsis drawn from the oral history of the Saxons and their struggles with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>. Its terseness makes it hard to interpret. Both Otto and his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a> are named Imperatores, yet it does not mention Otto's imperial coronation by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-xii">Pope John XII</a> on 2 February 962, and the text never names a pope.</p><p>The first book covers the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a>' early dukes and the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a>. The second opens with the election of Otto the Great as German king, treats the risings against him and ends with the death of his first wife Edith in 946. The third deals with Otto's expedition into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, his troubles with his son Liudolf and with Conrad, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>, and wars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> was first published in Basel in 1532; two others survive, and the best edition is that of Paul Hirsch and Hans-Eberhard Lohmann of 1935.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Widukind of Corvey” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kings endowed monasteries and convents such as Quedlinburg, Gandersheim and Corvey, which became centres of learning: the canoness Hrotsvitha of Gandersheim wrote Latin plays and poems in the tenth century, and the monk <b>Widukind of Corvey</b> wrote a history of the Saxons.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Harald’s conversion, usually dated to about 965 and linked by the chronicler <b>Widukind of Corvey</b> to the missionary Poppo, tied the kingdom to the German church.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Widukind of Corvey</b> reports that he killed some thirty Slavic chiefs at a banquet.</blockquote><a href="/state/marca-geronis">Marca Geronis</a></li><li><blockquote>Corvey sent Ansgar on his mission to Denmark and Sweden, and its school produced <b>Widukind of Corvey</b>, whose Deeds of the Saxons is a chief source for the Ottonian kings.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-abbey-of-corvey">Prince-Abbey of Corvey</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Princely Abbey of Corvey. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51,10&amp;year=956" aria-label="Germany on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4777 395 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Widukind of Corvey"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5227 665h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5227 665h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51,10&amp;year=956">What was here in 956 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
